# Client-side secrets

`secret-guard` and `security-secrets.mdc` protect the **repository**: keys in git, keystores, PEMs,
service-account JSON. Nothing protected the **APK**. A slot that compiled a provider key into
`app/src/main/`, or that depended on a provider SDK whose only authentication is a server-side
secret, would pass every gate the factory had and ship the credential to anyone who unzips the
artifact. No slot has done it yet; the gate exists so that "yet" stays true.

The measured exposure, from external research recorded in
`docs/research/2026-09-29-app-factory-prior-art.md` §3 row 8 and its source table (**ON RECORD**;
change **B1**, the highest-value change in that document):

- Of 206,867 real Android apps scanned, 1,207 were LLM-enabled and **66.3 % carried at least one
  LLM-API misuse** — **41.0 % leaked API credentials**, 24.3 % exposed system prompts, 39.1 % failed
  input sanitisation. Bypassing usage restrictions succeeded in **127 of 181 apps**, and 75 % of
  those attacks caused direct financial damage (arXiv 2505.08204, LM-Scout).
- Secrets were found in **35 % of 50,000 Play apps** collected Aug–Oct 2025 — 18,908 secrets, **1,802
  verified live** (Stripe keys, GitHub tokens, AI API keys).
- Roughly **1 in 4 AI-generated code samples** carries a confirmed OWASP vulnerability.

## The bar

**No shipped slot calls a paid API from the client.** A slot that needs a paid capability either

- pays nothing — the capability is offline by construction, or built from on-device logic; or
- does not ship. A capability that only works behind a server the factory does not build makes the
  project a server project, not a factory slot. Say that at stage 0, before the idea is approved,
  not after the first 401.

A secret's only legitimate home in a slot is a value the app never needs at runtime: a `.env` entry
the build reads to *configure a server the factory does not build*, or a keystore password. **Nowhere
in `app/src/main/`** — not in Kotlin, not in `strings.xml`, not in `res/xml/`, not in a JSON asset,
not in `libs.versions.toml`, not in the ProGuard rules, not in `google-services.json`. Anything under
`app/src/main/` is inside the APK and can be read by anyone who installs it; obfuscation and R8 do not
change that. A classpath dependency on a provider that authenticates with a secret is the same defect
one step earlier: the key will be needed somewhere, and the only place left is the client.

The consumer-safe exceptions are real and named: a Firebase API key shipped in
`google-services.json` is restricted by package name and SHA-1, and Stripe's **publishable** key is
client-safe. Neither licence is automatic — the detector fails them both, and the exemption file
below is where a slot says, in writing, why one is safe here.

## What is banned

- A provider key literal in the client sources — Google/Firebase (`AIza…`), OpenAI (`sk-`,
  `sk-proj-`), Anthropic (`sk-ant-`), Stripe (`sk_live_`, `sk_test_`, `rk_live_`), AWS (`AKIA`,
  `ASIA`), Slack (`xox[baprs]-`), GitHub (`ghp_`, `gho_`, `github_pat_`), a private-key PEM header,
  or any 20+ character value assigned to a name like `apiKey` / `api_key` / `secret` / `token` /
  `accessKey` / `clientSecret`.
- A dependency on `com.google.ai.client.generativeai`, `com.google.firebase:firebase-ai`,
  `com.openai`, `com.anthropic`, `com.stripe`, `com.amazonaws`, or a `play-services-mlkit-*` artifact
  that needs a key — declared in `app/build.gradle.kts`, in `gradle/libs.versions.toml`, or in the
  root `build.gradle.kts`.
- A raw call to `generativelanguage.googleapis.com`, `api.openai.com`, `api.anthropic.com`,
  `api.stripe.com`, `dashscope` or `openrouter.ai` from Kotlin or Java.
- A `google-services.json` carrying a non-placeholder key.
- A key assembled from literals to look less like a key. `"AIza" + "…"` is the same credential as the
  joined literal, and the sensor reads the line both ways for exactly that reason.

## The escape hatch is a file, not a comment

A match is never silenced by a marker in the scanned text, because the agent the scanner judges is the
agent that would write the marker. Two legitimate paths, both visible:

- A value that is a documented placeholder (`YOUR_API_KEY`, `TODO`, `xxxxxxxx`, `example`,
  `placeholder`, `<...>`) is **downgraded, not dropped**: it prints as a note and never fails.
- A whole file, for one named rule, may be exempted in
  `apps/<slot>/client-secrets-exempt.json` — an array of `{ path, rule, reason, decidedBy }`. Every
  applied exemption is printed with its path and rule, an entry with no `reason` or no `decidedBy` is
  a **failure**, and an entry that matches nothing is reported so it can be removed.

## How it is verified

```bash
node scripts/check-client-secrets.mjs --slot <slug> # 0 PASS, 1 FAIL, 2 UNVERIFIED
node scripts/test-client-secrets.mjs # offline sensor suite; a clean run is not an approval
```

- `scripts/lib/client-secrets.mjs` is the sensor; `scripts/check-client-secrets.mjs` is the gate, and
  it is the only thing that measures this rule. **If it is not in the run you are reading, the rule is
  unenforced for that run** — a rule that only a document asserts is this repository's most-recorded
  failure mode.
- A slot with no `app/src/main/` is `UNVERIFIED` (exit 2), never a pass: nothing scanned must not read
  as nothing found.
- The sensor reads `app/src/main/` for literals, the three build files for provider SDKs, the slot's
  ProGuard rules, and any `google-services.json`; it skips `build/`, so a stale generated copy of a
  source file cannot produce a finding.
- `scripts/test-client-secrets.mjs` carries the negative controls that keep it trustworthy —
  `val apiKey = "YOUR_API_KEY"` and the word "secret" in prose must not fire — and the positive ones a
  naive detector misses: a key split across literals, a key in `strings.xml`, in `libs.versions.toml`,
  in a `.pro` file, a keyed `google-services.json`, and a plaintext `api.openai.com` call.

What the sensor cannot claim: that an APK is secret-free. A key fetched at launch, assembled from
three variables, or hidden in a dependency's own resources is beyond a line-oriented scan. This rule
exists to make the common, measured defect unshippable — not to certify the artifact.
